Inheritance :

    Inheritance is one of the four pillars of Object-Oriented Programming.

    It allows one class to acquire the properties (variables) and behaviors (methods) of another class.

    Instead of writing the same code again and again, Java allows us to reuse existing classes.

    Inheritance represents an IS-A Relationship.

    Dog IS-A Animal

    Car IS-A Vehicle


Imagine every animal has

    name
    age
    eat()
    sleep()

Instead of writing these members in every class

    Dog

    Cat

    Lion

    Tiger

We write them only once.

    Animal

    All other classes inherit them.

    This reduces duplication.

Why do we need Inheritance?

Without Inheritance

Dog

    name
    age
    eat()

Cat

    name
    age
    eat()

Lion

    name
    age
    eat()

Everything is repeated.


With Inheritance

    Animal

    name
    age
    eat()

    ↓

    Dog

    ↓

    Cat

    ↓

    Lion

Only write common code once.

Advantages

    Code Reusability
    Easy Maintenance
    Less Code
    Better Readability
    Supports Runtime Polymorphism


Syntax
    class Child extends Parent{

    }

    Child class inherits Variables, Methods from Parent.